00:00:05.612 --> 00:00:07.320
SPEAKER: Cloud Shell
Editor makes it easy

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.403
for you to write code
directly in your web browser

00:00:09.403 --> 00:00:11.670
and deploy it to the
Cloud, and Cloud Run

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.620
makes it easy to
deploy highly scalable

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.329
containerized applications
on a fully managed serverless

00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:17.700
platform.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.210
So you might be wondering, can I
write the code for my Cloud Run

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.040
apps in Cloud Shell Editor?

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.500
Yes, you definitely can.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.500
Thanks to its Cloud Code
integration, Cloud Shell Editor

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.840
works great with Cloud
Run development workflows,

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.930
allowing you to build, deploy,
and debug your application

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:35.610
in just a couple of clicks.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:37.290
In this video,
we're going to see

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.170
how to use Cloud Shell Editor to
deploy an application to Cloud

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:40.980
Run.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.390
Let's go.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:45.384
First, visit
ide.cloud.google.com

00:00:45.384 --> 00:00:47.580
to launch the
Cloud Shell Editor.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.780
On the status bar at the
bottom of your screen,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.870
click Cloud Code.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.360
From the Cloud Code menu,
click New Application,

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.280
and then select the
Cloud Run application

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.340
for the type of sample
app we want to create.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.860
After that, we'll get a
list of Cloud Run templates

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.220
to choose from.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.770
Let's go with the
Python Flask option.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:08.490
Next, select a folder in
your Cloud Shell environment

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:11.190
to save your application
to, and give it a name.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.040
If that looks good, click
Create New Application.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.690
Cloud Shell Editor
clones the files

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:19.610
for your sample application from
GitHub into a new workspace.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:22.230
You can see the list of files
in the Explorer panel located

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.010
on the left side of the screen.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:26.770
Now let's go ahead and
deploy the service.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:30.600
Let's go down to the status
bar again and click Cloud Code.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.500
This time, we'll click
Deploy to Cloud Run.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.560
Click Authorize to authorize
Cloud Shell to make Google

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.280
Cloud Platform API calls to
give Cloud Shell Editor access

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.110
to your Google Cloud resource.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:44.700
Since this is the
first time using

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.830
Cloud Run in this project,
we're prompted here

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:50.880
to enable the Cloud Run API.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.090
In the Deploy to Cloud Run
dialog, under Service Settings,

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.590
we can set the name
for our service.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.840
And under Deployment,
we can select the region

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.490
we want it to be deployed to.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.350
Since we want to make
this a public service,

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.975
we'll keep Allow Unauthenticated
Invocations selected.

00:02:07.243 --> 00:02:08.660
There are some
additional settings

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.910
we can adjust, like container
settings, environment

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:15.440
variables, and Cloud
SQL connections.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:16.970
There are also build
settings, where

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:20.630
you can choose your builder
from Docker and Buildpacks.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.880
For now, we'll leave those
as their default values

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:24.450
and click Deploy.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.270
Cloud Code now
builds your image,

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:28.190
pushes it to the
Container Registry,

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:30.323
and deploys your
app to Cloud Run.

00:02:30.323 --> 00:02:32.240
Once you see the message
"Deployment completed

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.910
successfully," your service
is live and accessible

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:37.325
via the URL displayed in the
Deploy to Cloud Run dialog.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.850
That was pretty easy, and
updating your Cloud Run app

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:47.150
is just as simple.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:50.780
Let's redeploy your app with
an update to this message.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:53.660
Back in our Cloud Shell Editor
from the Explorer panel,

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:54.950
let's open the file app.py.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.550
In the hello function, we'll
find the message variable

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:04.682
that holds the friendly
HTTP greeting we just saw.

00:03:04.682 --> 00:03:05.765
Let's update that message.

00:03:08.972 --> 00:03:12.410
Cloud Shell Editor automatically
saves all file changes.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:15.500
Once we make our changes, we
can head back down to Cloud Code

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.230
down in the status bar.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:22.220
Once again, let's click
Deploy to Cloud Run.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.290
This time, the service
we created before

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:26.270
is listed here in
the Service Settings.

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:29.630
Let's hit Deploy to deploy
our updated service.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:32.780
Once it's done, we can head
back to our deployment URL

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:33.920
and refresh the page.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:37.850
And there it is.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:41.528
Our app has been redeployed
with the updated code.

00:03:41.528 --> 00:03:43.070
With just a couple
of clicks, we were

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:46.280
able to deploy, update, and
redeploy a simple Cloud Run

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:49.610
application, and we were
able to do it all directly

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:52.310
from our browser window without
having to leave our Cloud Shell

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:53.870
Editor.

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:55.460
Do you want to give
it a try yourself?

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:56.990
Check out the
interactive tutorial

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:59.330
linked in the description
below, or by launching it

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:01.610
from the Cloud Shell
Editor Welcome page.

